======================================================================
  PHASE 1: Intent Translation & Contract Deployment
======================================================================

  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue_index (upper_bound), ambient_noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: operator_fatigue_index <= 0.4, ambient_noise_level <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority list contains 'Throughput' three times, but no KPI named 'Throughput' exists in the contract — the actual objectives are resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue_index, and ambient_noise_level. Which ordering did you intend: (a) resource_efficiency first, fatigue second, noise third, or (b) a different ranking?
       A: Priority order is reset to [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue_index, ambient_noise_level]. Hard constraints (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB) are treated as inviolable; within the feasible region, resource_efficiency is maximised. This assumption will be flagged for mandatory manager confirmation before the optimiser runs.
    2. resource_efficiency / resource_efficiency_formula
       Q: The denominator sums energy (kWh), raw materials (kg), labour time (hours), and machine runtime (hours) into one number without unit-conversion weights — meaning the optimiser could legally slash labour hours by 10 % and triple energy consumption and still record a 'higher efficiency'. What are the relative importance weights (or monetary costs) for each resource type so the aggregation is dimensionally consistent?
       A: Each resource type is converted to a monetary cost using standard site tariffs (energy: local kWh rate, materials: BOM unit cost, labour: hourly wage, machine: depreciation rate). If tariffs are unavailable, equal unit-normalised weights are applied and a warning is raised. No individual resource may increase more than 20 % above its shift baseline in the optimised plan.
    3. resource_efficiency (maximize) — missing minimum throughput floor
       Q: Maximising value-per-resource-unit has a degenerate optimum: the optimiser can produce a small number of very high-value units with minimal resource use and report excellent efficiency while leaving most of the shift idle. Is there a minimum delivered output volume (e.g. ≥ 85 % of the baseline shift quota) that must also be met?
       A: A soft lower bound of 80 % of the historical average shift output is added as a penalised constraint. If no historical baseline is available, the bound defaults to 75 % of the planned shift order quantity. This prevents degenerate low-volume solutions while preserving efficiency optimisation.
