  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue (minimize), noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: operator_fatigue <= 0.4, noise_level <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority_order list contains 'Throughput' three times instead of the actual KPI names (resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, noise_level). When these objectives conflict—e.g., a faster cycle rate improves resource_efficiency but pushes operator_fatigue to exactly 0.4 or noise to exactly 80 dB—which KPI should the optimiser sacrifice first?
       A: Default priority order: (1) noise_level hard constraint honoured absolutely, (2) operator_fatigue hard constraint honoured absolutely, (3) resource_efficiency maximised subject to both constraints. No further tiebreak is applied beyond constraint satisfaction.
    2. resource_efficiency / kpi_targets[0].threshold (missing minimum throughput floor)
       Q: Is there a minimum number of units or a minimum value-output level the cell must deliver this shift, regardless of how good the efficiency ratio is? Without a floor, the optimiser could legally produce a small batch of high-value, low-resource items and idle the rest of the shift, maximising the ratio while leaving capacity unused—would that be acceptable?
       A: Assume no minimum absolute throughput floor; the optimiser targets the highest achievable resource_efficiency ratio across the full scheduled shift window without an output-quantity constraint.
    3. assumptions.resource_efficiency_operationalisation (cost coefficients undefined)
       Q: What are the relative cost weights for energy (kWh), material (kg), and labour-hours when computing total resource consumption? For example, if energy costs €0.15/kWh, material €5/kg, and labour €20/hr, the optimiser may aggressively cut labour hours at the expense of higher machine energy draw—is that substitution acceptable, and by how much?
       A: Apply equal normalised unit-cost weighting (1:1:1 after normalisation by typical shift averages for each resource stream) until explicit cost coefficients are supplied by the manager or the plant finance system.
    4. operator_fatigue (individual vs. aggregate scope in multi-operator cell)
       Q: Does the fatigue hard constraint of ≤ 0.4 apply to each individual operator (worst-case across the team), or to the team average? Enforcing the worst-case individual limit typically constrains job-rotation scheduling significantly and may reduce achievable resource_efficiency by 15–25% compared to an average-based rule.
       A: Apply the 0.4 limit to the maximum (worst-case) fatigue index across all operators in the cell; no single operator may exceed the threshold regardless of the team average, consistent with individual occupational health liability.
    5. noise_level (LAeq averaging window unspecified)
       Q: Is the 80 dB LAeq limit enforced as a rolling short-window average (e.g. every 15 minutes) or as a single cumulative average over the full 8-hour shift? A shift-long average permits transient spikes well above 80 dB early in the shift as long as the final mean is compliant, which may be unsafe even if technically within the stated standard.
       A: Enforce 80 dB LAeq as a rolling 15-minute equivalent average throughout the shift, consistent with the precautionary interpretation of EU Directive 2003/10/EC, to prevent sustained supra-threshold exposure in any short window.

