  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Deploy Supplementary Workforce: Bring in temporary, contract, or cross-trained operators to absorb the 20% demand increase. Distribute the additional workload across new shift slots or a parallel line, keeping each individual operator's fatigue score within the 0.40 ceiling. This fully preserves the human-centric constraint and meets demand without any schedule compromise. Lead time is 1–2 days to onboard temporary staff or activate cross-training rosters.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Stagger Demand Fulfillment Across Extended Scheduling Horizon: Accept the full 20% demand increase but spread it across an extended production window (e.g., add one additional shift cycle or extend the planning horizon by one period). Operators continue at normal loading; no individual fatigue threshold is breached. Partial deliveries are made on the original due date with the remainder fulfilled in the next window. Requires customer or downstream negotiation for a partial shipment arrangement.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Controlled Temporary Relaxation of Fatigue Limit with Enhanced Monitoring: Temporarily raise the operator_fatigue ceiling from 0.40 to 0.50 for this scheduling cycle only. This closes the -0.2001 gap sufficiently to produce a feasible schedule meeting full demand. Mandatory compensatory conditions must accompany this relaxation: (a) enhanced real-time fatigue monitoring via wearables or check-in protocols, (b) mandatory 15-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes, (c) prohibition on consecutive shifts above 0.45 fatigue, and (d) automatic reversion to 0.40 in the following cycle. This option relaxes a human-centric constraint and requires explicit sign-off from both the production manager and the safety officer before activation.
