       A: Uniform dimensionless weights (1:1:1) are applied as declared, which biases the optimizer toward reducing whichever resource has the highest physical quantity rather than highest economic cost. A warning flag is emitted to the planner before the solve.
    3. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed (maximize, no floor)
       Q: Maximizing a ratio with no minimum absolute throughput means the optimizer could legally satisfy the contract by producing a single high-value item while leaving the line largely idle. Is there a minimum delivered-value or minimum units-produced floor for the shift (e.g., ≥ 80% of standard shift output)?
       A: A soft lower bound of 75% of the rolling 30-day average shift output is applied to delivered value, treated as a penalty term with weight 10× the objective, to prevent degenerate low-volume solutions.
    4. operator_fatigue_index (hard constraint ≤ 0.4 vs. throughput maximization)
       Q: The fatigue hard constraint (≤ 0.4) is evaluated in real-time, but if the optimizer raises cycle rate to improve value-per-resource, fatigue typically accumulates nonlinearly across a shift — reaching 0.4 around hour 5–6 at elevated pace. Should the constraint be applied as a point-in-time limit (triggering an immediate slowdown) or as a shift-average limit, and is there a maximum sustained cycle-rate multiplier (e.g., ≤ 1.15× nominal) to prevent oscillating speed changes?
       A: Constraint is enforced as a real-time upper bound; if the predicted fatigue trajectory breaches 0.4 before shift end, the optimizer rolls back cycle rate to the last feasible level. No explicit cycle-rate multiplier cap is applied unless supplied.
    5. ambient_noise_level (KPI threshold vs. hard-constraint operator mismatch)
       Q: The KPI declares threshold = 80.0 dB (allowing equality) while the hard constraint uses strict '<' 80.0 dB; additionally, the assumption states LAeq (time-averaged), but individual machine bursts can transiently exceed 80 dB even when the shift average is compliant. Which governs: (a) shift-level LAeq strictly < 80 dB, (b) LAeq ≤ 80 dB, or (c) no instantaneous reading above 80 dB?
       A: The stricter hard-constraint operator ('<') takes precedence; the limit is interpreted as shift-level LAeq < 80 dB to align with EU Directive 2003/10/EC, with real-time monitoring alerting (but not immediately halting) on transient peaks pending a 15-minute rolling average check.

