  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Authorize Temporary Overtime (Bounded): Permit a single daily overtime extension of up to 1 hour per affected shift for a maximum of 10 consecutive working days. This adds roughly 12.5% effective capacity on constrained shifts without altering core rest or ergonomic limits. Requires explicit manager sign-off each day and mandatory review at day 5. No change to mandatory rest intervals or weekend scheduling.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Add a Third Micro-Shift (Partial Staffing): Introduce a supplemental 4-hour production window staffed by voluntary part-time or cross-trained personnel. This distributes the 15% demand increase across a new time block rather than extending existing shifts, preserving all per-worker hour and ergonomic limits. Requires HR coordination, a minimum of 72 hours lead time, and confirmation of cross-training certification for assigned workers.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Partial Demand Deferral with Customer Notification: Defer approximately 10–12% of the demand increase to the next scheduling horizon by renegotiating delivery windows with affected customers. This keeps the production schedule fully within all existing human-centric constraints and requires no workforce policy changes. It trades on-time delivery performance for full regulatory and ethical compliance. Recommended only if Option 1 and Option 2 are operationally unavailable.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes

