    - Option 2 — Partial Demand Deferral (90% fulfilment now, 10% deferred to next cycle): Accept and schedule 90% of the incremental demand increase immediately under the existing compliant workforce configuration, and defer the remaining ~8-9% of orders to the next production cycle. Simulation shows that a 10% volume reduction brings operator fatigue to approximately 0.387 (within the 0.40 limit) and noise to approximately 79.4 dB (within the 80.0 dB limit), restoring full constraint compliance. This option preserves all human-centric limits and requires no engineering changes, but introduces a delivery gap for lower-priority orders. Recommended when supplementary staffing is not immediately available. Customer communication and order re-sequencing by planning team required within 2 hours.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Time-Bounded Fatigue Constraint Relaxation with Mandatory Compensatory Protocol: Temporarily raise the operator fatigue threshold from 0.40 to 0.62 for a maximum of one 8-hour shift, conditioned on mandatory: (a) 15-minute paid rest breaks every 90 minutes, (b) noise PPE (EN 352-certified earmuffs, NRR ≥ 25 dB) for all floor personnel for the duration, and (c) post-shift occupational health check-in. This allows the full 15% demand increase to be processed in a single cycle. The noise constraint is not formally relaxed but is partially mitigated by PPE; engineering controls (acoustic panels, equipment sequencing) should be scheduled within 5 business days to eliminate recurrence. This option relaxes a worker-protection constraint and must be approved by the occupational safety officer and documented per site regulatory requirements before implementation. Use only if Options 1 and 2 are both infeasible.
