  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority_order field lists ['Throughput','Throughput','Throughput'] but 'Throughput' is not a defined KPI — the objective is 'resource_efficiency'. Should the priority order be ['resource_efficiency','operator_fatigue','noise_level'], or is there a separate throughput KPI that was omitted from kpi_targets?
       A: Priority order is reassigned to ['resource_efficiency','operator_fatigue','noise_level'] matching the defined KPIs in lexicographic contract order; no standalone throughput KPI is added unless the manager confirms one is required.
    2. resource_efficiency (maximize, no floor on output)
       Q: Maximising value/resource as an unconstrained ratio allows the optimiser to achieve a high score by producing very little output at very low resource cost — is there a minimum output volume (e.g. ≥ 80% of the shift's nominal production target) below which the solution is unacceptable?
       A: A minimum throughput floor of 80% of the observed shift-start nominal rate is applied as an implicit hard constraint to prevent the optimiser from gaming the ratio by starving production.
    3. resource_efficiency (maximize, no resource cap)
       Q: Without an upper bound on total resource draw, maximising efficiency can still cause the optimiser to ramp up energy or labour consumption by ~30–40% if the value numerator grows faster than the cost denominator — should total energy consumption and/or labour-hours be capped at, say, 110% of the shift baseline?
       A: Total resource consumption (energy + materials + labour-hours, in their native units) is each individually capped at 115% of the shift-start baseline values to prevent unsafe over-draw while maintaining optimisation headroom.
    4. context.temporal_scope / operator_fatigue threshold
       Q: The fatigue hard constraint is ≤ 0.4 (normalised) but shift duration is unspecified; a 12-hour shift makes fatigue ≤ 0.4 far harder to sustain than an 8-hour shift and may render the contract infeasible — what is the planned shift length in hours?
       A: An 8-hour shift is assumed; if actual shift length is longer the fatigue ceiling is relaxed linearly to 0.35 × (8 / shift_hours) to preserve the same safety margin relative to shift exposure time.
    5. assumptions.resource_definition (aggregate weights)
       Q: 'Resource' is defined as an aggregate of energy, materials, and labour-hours, but no exchange weights or normalisation coefficients are specified — if the optimiser converts all three to a single scalar using uncalibrated weights it may trade off materials for energy in ways management did not intend; can you supply relative cost weights (e.g. energy cost per kWh, material cost per kg, labour cost per hour)?
       A: Each resource dimension is normalised to its shift-start consumption (dimensionless fraction of baseline) and summed with equal weight (1/3 each); this is flagged as a high-uncertainty assumption and the sensitivity of the optimal solution to weight perturbations is reported alongside the schedule.

