  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 15.0% demand surge has rendered the proposed production schedule infeasible under current human-centric safety constraints. Two violations were detected: (1) Operator fatigue index reached 0.6001, exceeding the maximum allowable threshold of 0.40 by a margin of 0.2001 — a 50% overage that poses significant ergonomic and health risk; (2) Shop-floor noise level reached 84.9 dB, exceeding the 80.0 dB occupational safety limit by 4.9 dB. Because both violations directly affect worker wellbeing, the schedule has been rejected and escalated for human review. No automated override has been applied. The system estimates that absorbing the full 15% demand increase without any intervention is not possible within current constraint envelopes. Immediate remediation is required to resume scheduling.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Expand Workforce Capacity via Additional Shift or Temporary Labour: Introduce a second partial shift or onboard temporary contract operators to distribute the incremental 15% demand load across a larger labour pool. Fatigue is load-per-operator-hour; splitting production volume reduces individual operator exposure below the 0.40 threshold without changing the constraint. Simultaneously, staggering shift start times reduces concurrent machine operation density, naturally attenuating aggregate noise levels below 80.0 dB. No safety limits are relaxed. Estimated lead time: 3–5 business days for temp staffing. Slight coordination overhead may introduce a minor scheduling gap.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Engineering Controls — Acoustic Mitigation and Structured Job Rotation: Deploy acoustic barrier panels and anti-vibration mounts on the highest-noise workstations to achieve an estimated 3–5 dB reduction, bringing noise back to or near the 80.0 dB ceiling. Concurrently, implement a mandatory 90-minute operator rotation schedule to cap sustained fatigue accumulation; modelling suggests this reduces the fatigue index from 0.6001 to approximately 0.45–0.48 under full demand. A minor, time-limited relaxation of the noise threshold to 82.0 dB may still be required while acoustic hardware is installed (estimated 5–7 days). Operator hearing protection (PPE) must be mandatory during this interim window. This option partially preserves human-centric constraints and closes roughly 70% of the demand gap.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Temporary Supervised Constraint Relaxation with Enhanced Safety Protocol: As a time-bounded emergency measure (not to exceed 10 production days), raise the operator fatigue threshold to 0.50 and the noise ceiling to 85.0 dB, sufficient to accommodate the full 15% demand increase under the existing schedule. This option requires: (a) mandatory audiometric and fatigue monitoring every 4 hours; (b) full hearing-protection PPE enforcement; (c) written informed consent from affected operators; (d) daily sign-off by the site safety officer; and (e) automatic reinstatement of original thresholds at expiry. This approach fully closes the demand gap with zero scheduling delay but materially relaxes both human-centric constraints and must be treated as an exception, not a new baseline. Regulatory notification may be required depending on jurisdiction.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
