  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue (minimize), ambient_noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: operator_fatigue <= 0.4, ambient_noise_level <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority_order list contains ["Throughput","Throughput","Throughput"] — none of these entries map to any KPI name in the contract (resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, ambient_noise_level); when resource_efficiency gains conflict with fatigue or noise headroom, which KPI wins, and in what strict order (e.g., fatigue > noise > resource_efficiency)?
       A: Treat both hard constraints (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB(A)) as lexicographically prior to the optimisation objective; among the three KPIs apply priority order: (1) operator_fatigue, (2) ambient_noise_level, (3) resource_efficiency maximisation.
    2. resource_efficiency / minimum_output_floor
       Q: resource_efficiency is maximised with no minimum output floor, so the optimiser could legally achieve a high value-per-resource ratio by idling most machines and delivering very little — should a minimum delivered output level be enforced (e.g., ≥ 85 % of this shift's planned production target)?
       A: Apply a minimum output floor of 85 % of the shift's planned production target; if no target exists, use 80 % of the trailing 4-week average shift output for the same product mix.
    3. resource_efficiency / denominator_aggregation_weights
       Q: The efficiency denominator combines energy, raw materials, labour time, and machine time in 'normalised units' without explicit cost weights; the optimiser could exploit whichever resource is cheapest in normalised terms (e.g., trading overtime labour for energy savings in a ratio the manager did not intend) — what relative cost weight should each resource type carry (e.g., energy : materials : labour : machine-time = 1 : 3 : 2 : 1)?
       A: Use equal normalised weights (1:1:1:1) across all four resource types; flag any single-resource substitution exceeding 20 % of its baseline consumption for human review before the schedule is committed.
    4. ambient_noise_level / measurement_method
       Q: The 80 dB(A) constraint is enforced as a shift-long time-weighted average (TWA), which permits short high-noise bursts well above 80 dB(A) that are masked by quieter periods; EU Directive 2003/10/EC also specifies an upper exposure action value of 85 dB(A) LEX — should the optimiser additionally enforce a short-interval instantaneous ceiling (e.g., a 5-minute rolling Leq ≤ 85 dB(A)) to prevent TWA-averaging exploitation?
       A: Enforce both the 80 dB(A) shift-level TWA and a 5-minute rolling Leq ceiling of 85 dB(A); if sensor resolution does not support rolling Leq, apply an 83 dB(A) TWA ceiling as a conservative proxy.
    5. operator_fatigue / constraint_vs_kpi_conflict
       Q: Both a soft KPI (minimise fatigue) and a hard constraint (fatigue ≤ 0.4) target the same variable; in practice the optimiser will push fatigue to exactly 0.4, treating the hard limit as the de-facto operating point — should a separate soft KPI target be set (e.g., aim for ≤ 0.25 with 0.4 as the inviolable ceiling), or is any fatigue value up to 0.4 fully acceptable to the manager?
       A: Retain 0.4 as the inviolable hard constraint and add a soft KPI target of ≤ 0.30, weighted at 20 % of the resource_efficiency objective, so the optimiser prefers schedules with meaningful fatigue headroom rather than running operators at the regulatory limit.

