  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Expand Workforce Allocation via Supplementary Shift: Introduce a supplementary operator shift or call in cross-trained personnel to distribute the additional 25% demand load across a larger workforce pool. By increasing the number of active operators, per-operator task intensity and cumulative fatigue exposure are reduced proportionally. Noise-intensive machine operations should be staggered across shifts rather than run concurrently to keep ambient dB within limits. This is the preferred path as it meets demand without compromising any safety standard and requires only operational scheduling changes.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Partial Demand Deferral to Next Production Cycle: Defer approximately 18-20% of the demand increment to the next available production window, scheduling only the volume that can be fulfilled within current fatigue and noise constraints. This keeps operator safety fully intact and avoids any regulatory exposure. The trade-off is a partial on-time delivery shortfall for the deferred portion. Recommended when delivery deadlines for the incremental demand have sufficient float, or when customer agreements allow partial-period fulfillment. Coordinate with supply chain and customer-facing teams before authorizing.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Time-Limited Supervised Constraint Relaxation with Mandatory Controls: Authorize a temporary, tightly governed relaxation of both violated constraints for a single production cycle only: operator_fatigue ceiling raised to 0.50 (from 0.40) and noise_level ceiling raised to 82.0 dB (from 80.0 dB). This must be paired with mandatory countermeasures: enforced micro-break schedules every 45 minutes, mandatory PPE (noise-attenuating earmuffs rated to bring effective exposure below 78 dB), real-time biometric fatigue monitoring with automatic line-stop authority, and a post-cycle medical check-in for all affected operators. This option must NOT be repeated in consecutive cycles without reassessment. Human-centric constraints are formally relaxed; regulatory and liability implications must be reviewed by EHS before authorization.
      Preserves human constraints: No

